Effective with Version 3.6.0, the format of the suckkillfile has changed.
I did this because I kept getting requests to add the ability to scan for
different headers, and the section of the code that was doing the scanning
was getting bigger (and slower). 

Gone are: FROM=, PATH=, SUBJECT=, NNTPHOST= and the Separators.  Instead
I have replaced them with the ability to scan any header line.  Also, 
each search pattern must be on a separate line.  Example:

FROM=boby,anyone,noone  would be replaced with

From:boby
From:anyone
From:noone

Note that the header field matches what is seen in a message.  So if you
wanted to scan on the Followup-To field, you'd have to say

Followup-To:

Also, the start of the line for the scan is the first character after the
space following the header

From: boby@pixi.com

The first character scanned is the first 'b' in boby.

Options LOWLINES=, HILINES=, NRGRPS=, QUOTE=, and GROUP=  stayed the same.

New options are:

HEADER:, GROUP_OVERRIDE_MASTER, and TIEBREAKER_DELETE

HEADER: allows you to scan the entire header, instead of just one header line.

GROUP_OVERRIDE_DELETE allows you to have actions in the group files override
the actions from master.  In previous versions, if a message matched in the
master file, the group files were never even checked.  This options says hey,
even if you get flagged for delete, check the group files.  If a group file
says keep, then keep it.  

TIEBREAKER_DELETE

This takes the place of the #define in suck_config.h.  The default for suck
is if a message hits on both a group keep and group delete, then to keep it.
This option tells suck to delete it instead of keeping it.

Both GROUP_OVERRIDE_DELETE, and TIEBREAKER_DELETE are valid only in the
master killfile (suckkillfile).  They are ignored in any other files.

I added a check to file-parsing routine, so that if an expression is a 
string, and not a regular expression (assuming you have regex on your system),
then we don't use the regex stuff.  Hopefully this will make the scanning faster.
I detect a regular expression by scanning for certain characters in the string.
If I detect any of the following characters *[]()^$\. then I assume a regex.  If
I accidently left out a special character, add it to rege_chars[] in killfile.c, 
and let me know, so I can add it into the next version. 

I also added some options to the logging process.  There are now three options:

-L  - same as before, no logging
-LL - LONG version, show reason killed and header (same as before)  DEFAULT
-LS - SHORT version, just show 1 line reason why killed.

I added some debugging printouts to the -D option, in case of problems.

Added -k option so that when suck parses the killfiles, it doesn't put the postfix
on the name of the files it tries to match.  This is so you can maintain one set
of killfiles for multiple servers.
